@@s001 Ford-Fulkerson: the maximum flow
@@s002 ora:augmenting path,ok:saturated arc (flow = capacity),no:cancelled flow (residual network),dato:available capacity
@@s003 Network: each arc has <b>capacity 1</b>, written as <code>flow/capacity</code>. Initial flow 0. I want to send as much flow as possible from <b>s</b> to <b>t</b>.
@@s004 First path: <b>s → a → b → t</b>. The bottleneck is 1: I increase the flow by 1 on these three arcs. Now they are saturated.
@@s005 I look for another path in the <b>residual network</b>. On s → a → b nothing passes any more. But the arc <b>b → a</b> in the residual network is the arc a → b cancelled: I can "undo" the flow.
@@s006 Second path: <b>s → b → a → t</b>. I pass through b → a: the flow on a → b goes back to 0. I add <b>s → b</b> and <b>a → t</b>.
@@s007 No path s → t in the residual network: the flow is <b>maximum</b>. Value 2. The cut {s} has capacity 2: see the max-flow min-cut page.
@@s008 Ford-Fulkerson: the maximum flow
@@s009 The maximum-flow problem: sending as much material as possible from a source s to a sink t, respecting the capacities of the arcs. Ford-Fulkerson solves it with augmenting paths in the residual network.
@@s010 The network and the flow
@@s011 A <b>network</b> is a directed graph with a source <code>s</code>, a sink <code>t</code> and a capacity <code>c(e) ≥ 0</code> on each arc. A <b>flow</b> <code>f</code> assigns to each arc a value with <code>0 ≤ f(e) ≤ c(e)</code>, and at each node other than s and t what enters equals what leaves (conservation). The value of the flow is what leaves <code>s</code>.
@@s012 The residual network
@@s013 For each arc <code>e = u → v</code> with capacity <code>c</code> and flow <code>f</code>, the <b>residual network</b> contains: a forward arc <code>u → v</code> with capacity <code>c − f</code> (how much you can still send), and a backward arc <code>v → u</code> with capacity <code>f</code> (how much you can <b>undo</b>). The backward arc is what allows correcting a choice made before.
@@s014 The method: augmenting paths
@@s015 While there is a path <code>s → t</code> in the residual network, take its <b>bottleneck</b> (the minimum capacity along the path) and increase the flow by that amount. To find the path: BFS or DFS. When there is no path any more, the flow is maximum.
@@s016 First path, then a path that undoes
@@s017 The theorem and the cost
@@s018 <b>Augmenting path theorem</b>: a flow is maximum if and only if there is no path <code>s → t</code> in the residual network. <b>Integrality</b>: with integer capacities there is an integer maximum flow, and Ford-Fulkerson finds it. That is why a 0/1 flow is read as an assignment.
@@s019 Cost <code>O(m · |f*|)</code>: with integer capacities it depends on the value of the flow. With capacity 1: <code>O(mn)</code>.
@@s020 The code, side by side
@@s021 This page explains the concept. The Java code that implements it, with a line-by-line explanation and the syntax notes needed, is on the side-by-side page.
